## Host/View contract

The host owns loading, persistence, save/submit and dialog lifecycle.

The View receives exactly:

```js
{
  decisionContext,
  evaluation,
  setEvaluation,
  collectiveEvaluation,
  readOnly,
}
```

There is no `loading` prop.

Treat `evaluation` as immutable.

`setEvaluation(nextEvaluation)` replaces the complete payload; it is not a patch
API.

`collectiveEvaluation` may be null and is read-only.

When `readOnly` is true, prevent every mutating interaction.

Use data already in `decisionContext`; do not fetch it again.

## Expression Domains

If the user enters a value belonging to `criterion.expressionDomain`, reuse:

```js
import {
  ExpressionDomainEvaluationInput,
  validateExpressionDomainEvaluation,
} from "../../../../expressionDomains";
```

Use the canonical criterion domain. Do not recreate registered numeric,
linguistic, fuzzy or 2-tuple inputs/validation.


## CreteValleyDSS visual language

Use React and Material UI (`@mui/material`). The application already provides its
global `ThemeProvider`, `CssBaseline` and global styles. Do not create another
theme or design system inside the plugin.

The app supports both light and dark schemes. Do not force one mode.

Representative theme values:

```text
Typography
- Source Sans Pro, Arial, sans-serif
- base font size: 15px
- headings: bold
- buttons: bold

Light
- primary.main: #134F8A
- secondary.main / info.main: #45C5C5
- background.default: #F5F0F6
- background.paper: #FFFFFF
- text.primary: #1D1D1B
- text.secondary: #545454

Dark
- primary.main: #224261
- primary.light / secondary.main / info.main: #45C5C5
- background.default: #1D1D1B
- background.paper: #262B32
- text.primary: #FFFFFF
- text.secondary: #BFBFBF
```

Prefer semantic MUI values in `sx`, such as `background.paper`, `text.primary`,
`text.secondary`, `divider`, `secondary.main` and `info.main`, rather than
hard-coded colors.

Match the application's compact visual language:

- prefer `Stack`/`Box` compositions;
- modest spacing/padding, commonly around 1–1.5 theme units;
- compact plugin surfaces commonly use `borderRadius: 2`;
- use subtle borders/dividers;
- use `body2`/`caption` for supporting text;
- prefer compact buttons/toggles to oversized controls;
- use clear but restrained selected/active states;
- wrap responsively instead of assuming wide screens;
- avoid oversized cards, large empty areas and decorative UI that does not aid
  the task.

A subtle low-opacity accent may be used for grouping. Avoid excessive gradients,
glass effects or arbitrary bright colors.

Prefer MUI `sx`. If styling becomes large enough to obscure component logic,
extract it to `styles/<ComponentName>.styles.js`.

Do not introduce Tailwind, Bootstrap, styled-components, another component
library, a new ThemeProvider or a new global CSS system.

Do not rely on color alone to communicate validation, ordering or selection.



## React implementation style

Keep the state model simple.

- host-provided payload/value props are the source of truth;
- do not mirror props into local state without a real lifecycle reason;
- do not add `useEffect` just to synchronize derived values;
- derive inexpensive values directly;
- use `useMemo`/`useCallback` only when they solve a real problem;
- update objects/arrays immutably;
- do not create one-use custom hooks;
- do not fetch data already supplied in context;
- do not add third-party dependencies when React/MUI/platform APIs suffice;
- do not add compatibility aliases/fallback payloads unless explicitly required.


